WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.201
A safer string API makes the important
parts of the operation visible.

2
00:00:04.281 --> 00:00:08.609
It asks for destination capacity rather
than assuming the buffer is large enough.

3
00:00:08.689 --> 00:00:11.449
It distinguishes content length from
storage size.

4
00:00:11.529 --> 00:00:16.046
It reports whether the operation
succeeded, failed, or produced a truncated result.

5
00:00:16.126 --> 00:00:19.199
It documents who owns memory and what
cleanup is required.

6
00:00:19.279 --> 00:00:23.796
Explicit capacity parameters reduce
guesswork, but only when the value is accurate.

7
00:00:23.876 --> 00:00:27.828
Passing the size of a pointer instead of
the size of the buffer is still a bug.

8
00:00:27.908 --> 00:00:30.730
Passing an old capacity after a resize is
still wrong.

9
00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:32.943
Safer APIs improve the shape of the call,

10
00:00:33.023 --> 00:00:35.595
but the program must still track the real
object.

11
00:00:35.675 --> 00:00:37.933
Return values are central to safer design.

12
00:00:38.013 --> 00:00:41.839
A good API explains what success looks
like, how truncation is reported,

13
00:00:41.919 --> 00:00:45.809
what failure means, and whether it returns
required size, bytes written,

14
00:00:45.889 --> 00:00:47.457
status codes, or a new object.

15
00:00:47.537 --> 00:00:51.928
Code that ignores those results loses the
very safety signal the API was designed to

16
00:00:52.008 --> 00:00:52.510
provide.

17
00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:54.973
Termination behavior should be documented.

18
00:00:55.053 --> 00:00:59.570
Some APIs guarantee a terminator when the
destination capacity is greater than zero.

19
00:00:59.650 --> 00:01:00.402
Others do not.

20
00:01:00.482 --> 00:01:01.988
Some write a partial result.

21
00:01:02.068 --> 00:01:03.636
Others reject the operation.

22
00:01:03.716 --> 00:01:05.598
Guaranteed termination is useful,

23
00:01:05.678 --> 00:01:09.818
but only if the caller also handles
truncation and knows whether the resulting

24
00:01:09.898 --> 00:01:11.090
string is meaningful.

25
00:01:11.170 --> 00:01:13.114
Ownership rules matter just as much.

26
00:01:13.194 --> 00:01:16.895
A dynamic string API might allocate a new
buffer, grow an existing one,

27
00:01:16.975 --> 00:01:21.053
borrow a pointer, or return a view that
becomes invalid after the next append.

28
00:01:21.133 --> 00:01:24.708
Safer APIs should make these rules easy to
read and hard to overlook.

29
00:01:24.788 --> 00:01:27.799
The best string APIs make misuse easier to
spot in review.

30
00:01:27.879 --> 00:01:32.207
They have consistent names, clear argument
order, documented failure behavior,

31
00:01:32.287 --> 00:01:34.984
and project examples that show expected
handling.

32
00:01:35.064 --> 00:01:39.518
A library is safer when its contracts are
visible enough for ordinary maintainers to

33
00:01:39.598 --> 00:01:40.100
enforce.
